## Why this matters

Object shape changes silently destroy JavaScript performance. Hidden classes (maps) and inline caches explain why "just add a field later" can invalidate optimizations across hot loops.

## The simple version

Think of a hidden class as a warehouse's shelf plan: "aisle 1 holds `id`, aisle 2 holds `name`, aisle 3 holds `email`." As long as every box (object) follows the same shelf plan, a forklift (the CPU) can grab "aisle 2" without ever reading the label, it just knows where `name` lives. That's an inline cache: a memorized shortcut for "this shelf plan, this aisle."

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["object literal {id, name}"] --> B["Shelf plan A: id@1, name@2"]
  C["object literal {name, id}"] --> D["Shelf plan B: name@1, id@2"]
  B --> E[Forklift memorizes: aisle 2 = name]
  D --> F[Different plan -> forklift must re-check every time]
```

Where the analogy breaks: a real warehouse could just relabel the shelves once. V8 can't retroactively fix a shelf plan, the moment an object's property order or set of properties differs from what a call site has seen before, a *new* shelf plan is created and the old memorized shortcut no longer applies at that site. If a loop feeds the same code mixed shelf plans, the forklift keeps re-checking on every trip, which is far slower than sticking to one plan.

## Core mechanics

V8 assigns a hidden class to each object shape. Adding properties in different orders or deleting properties creates new maps. ICs remember where to load a property for a given map at a call site.

## Mental model

Treat objects like rows in a table with a fixed column order. Changing the schema mid-flight forces new rows and misses caches.

## Runnable experiment

```js
// Same shape every time (monomorphic)
function makeOrdered(id, name) { return { id, name }; }

// Shape varies by branch (polymorphic)
function makeMixed(id, name, withExtra) {
  const o = { id, name };
  if (withExtra) o.extra = true; // sometimes a 3rd shape
  return o;
}

const N = 2_000_000;
let t0 = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { const o = makeOrdered(i, 'a'); void o.name; }
console.log('monomorphic ms:', performance.now() - t0);

t0 = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { const o = makeMixed(i, 'a', i % 2 === 0); void o.name; }
console.log('polymorphic ms:', performance.now() - t0);
```

## Trade-offs and failure modes

Deleting a property (`delete obj.x`) or adding properties conditionally are the two most common ways production code accidentally creates multiple shapes at one call site, deoptimizing a hot loop that looked fine in isolation.

## Tool-building exercise

Write a tiny script using `process.memoryUsage` and a micro-benchmark harness to compare monomorphic vs polymorphic property access patterns.

## Key takeaways

- Property order defines hidden classes.
- ICs speed monomorphic access.
- Polymorphism deoptimizes hot paths.

